#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069927 :tutorial/content "To simplify a rational expression, we cancel out the largest common factor from the numerator and denominator. To do this, we sometimes need to factor the numerator and denominator before canceling.\n\nFor example, suppose that we wish to simplify\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {4x - 12} {10x + 4}\n$$\n\nTo do this, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any factors that they have in common.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac {4x - 12} {10x + 4} &=\\dfrac {4(x - 3)} {2(5x + 2)} \\\\[5pt] &=\\dfrac {2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot (x - 3)} {2(5x + 2)} \\\\[5pt] &=\\dfrac {\\cancel{2} \\cdot 2(x - 3)} {\\cancel{2}(5x + 2)} \\\\[5pt] &=\\dfrac {2(x - 3)} {5x + 2} \\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "332f534f-811e-5c03-8393-560134dd4ea8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1370 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
